{
  "_doc": "PERSONA (Phase 058): the data a resident's persona is built from. Read by domains/persona.py (the persona card, the usual day, the life sketch), domains/relationship_seeding.py (the founding ties) and domains/persona_check.py (the self-test). Tables: PERSONA_TRAITS (each trait of a founding resident, its drawn source and its lexicon quantity); PERSONA_ENGINE_PARAMS (the raw numbers that stay engine configuration, not traits); ROUTINE_BANDS (the four hour bands the simple backend reads, see bigville/backends/cheap.py); ROUTINE (the usual day of each job, adult; a '_child' and '_infant' row for dependants); ROUTINE_STAGE (life stage: which row and which overrides); ROUTINE_WEEK (market day and church day); ROUTINE_SEASON (the seasonal farm task); PLACE_WORDS (the words for each place in JOB_ROUTINE and the common places); BIOGRAPHY_TEMPLATES, YEARS_WORDS, SPAN_WORDS, SCHOOLING_WORDS, OFFICE_WORDS (the life sketch); RELATIONSHIP_SEEDING (counts, strengths and history lines for the founding ties).\n\nSOURCES AND APPROX. Hours and tasks in ROUTINE are APPROX, a model of a pre-industrial English village working day, not measured. The day is organised from the sun and a midday break, as E. P. Thompson describes preindustrial task and clock time in 'Time, Work-Discipline, and Industrial Capitalism' (Past and Present 38, 1967); the hour values are not taken from that article. The four simple-backend bands (9, 17, 21) are the boundaries cheap.py used before Phase 058, carried over unchanged. The market day and church day (weekday 5 and 6 of a 7-day week) are model choices (APPROX). The seasonal tasks are APPROX. Relationship counts follow Dunbar's layered networks (Dunbar 1993, Behavioral and Brain Sciences 16(4), the sympathy group of about 15; Hill and Dunbar 2003, Human Nature 14, the close circle) and are APPROX within that; the 12 close ties cap is a model choice. Dunbar's figures are recalled from those articles and were not checked against the text in this phase (no network access).\n\nTraits: each PERSONA_TRAITS key names the FOUNDING_PERSONALITY key it reads (or role_identity, which is an identity attribute) and the lexicon quantity whose words describe it. A trait with no 'other' words is seen by others not at all, as lexicon.json says.",
  "_types": {},
